-- The `equatable` walk of explicit `Basics.eq` does not accept a type
-- variable just because it sits inside a structure (checker-v2.md §11.4):
-- the rigid `a` inside `[ x ]` has no `equatable` flag, so `same` is
-- `not_equatable` in its body; the flex `x` inside `( x, 1 )` gets the flag,
-- so `same2` is inferred `∀(a: equatable)` and its call with two functions
-- is `not_equatable` — never a clean check whose built program compares
-- two functions at run time and prints `different` twice. The controls,
-- `Basics.eq x y` directly, are refused at these same positions (the first
-- argument, and the first argument of the call).

same : a, a -> Bool
same x y = Basics.eq [ x ] [ y ]


same2 x y = Basics.eq ( x, 1 ) ( y, 1 )


use : Bool
use = same2 (λn -> n + 1) (λn -> n + 1)
